IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g8-nouns`
IMPORT `g8-penalties`

-- g8-art26-demand: Article 26 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, the Commissioner's power to demand information, retention of what is provided, and the two offences.
-- A power is a MAY rule typed by who acts and what is done, with its preconditions as computable conditions; a discretion ("reasonably requires", "reasonable excuse",
-- "appears to be") is an input named for what it decides.  An offence is a prohibition with the penalty as data (g8-penalties).
-- Each rule that depends on a fork takes `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` and delegates by CONSIDER to one implementation per reading.
-- Every period of Article 26 has a fork: the year of 26(5) (F8.1, F8.9, F8.10), "as soon as practicable" in 26(6) (F8.2), the time a notice fixes (F8.7),
-- and the term of imprisonment in 26(7) and 26(10) (F8.8, in g8-penalties).

§ `Part 7 -- Information and enforcement`

§§ `Article 26 -- Power to demand information`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(2): the persons who can be demanded of
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(2) -- the persons falling within paragraph (2)`

-- src:621 | (2) The persons falling within this paragraph are –
-- src:622 | (a) a registered charity or an entity that was formerly a registered charity; and
-- src:623 | (b) a governor or former governor of a registered charity or of an entity that was formerly a registered charity.
@ref Article 26(2)(a) (src:622)
GIVEN person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of: how the person stands to the entity, and where the entity stands on the register
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(2)(a) the person is a registered charity or an entity that was formerly one` person MEANS
        person's `capacity of the person under Article 26(2)` EQUALS `the entity itself`
    AND `Art 26(2) the entity is or was a registered charity` person

-- "A registered charity" is an entity entered in the general or the restricted section (Article 1); an entity in the historic section was formerly registered,
-- and so was any entity the caller says was registered earlier (an applicant who was once a charity is "formerly" one).
@ref Article 26(2)(a) (src:622)
GIVEN person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(2) the entity is or was a registered charity` person MEANS
        person's `standing of the entity on the register` EQUALS `entered in the general section`
    OR  person's `standing of the entity on the register` EQUALS `entered in the restricted section`
    OR  person's `standing of the entity on the register` EQUALS `formerly registered and entered in the historic section`
    OR  person's `the entity was formerly a registered charity`

-- F8.17.  Reading A (taken): the text pairs "governor or former governor" with "a registered charity or an entity that was formerly a registered charity" and asks for no overlap
-- in time between the two.  Reading B asks that the person was a governor while the entity was registered.
@ref Article 26(2)(b) (src:623)
GIVEN person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(2)(b) reading A: a governor or former governor of an entity registered at any time` person MEANS
        person's `capacity of the person under Article 26(2)` EQUALS `a governor or former governor of the entity`
    AND `Art 26(2) the entity is or was a registered charity` person

@ref Article 26(2)(b) (src:623)
GIVEN person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(2)(b) reading B: a governor while the entity was registered` person MEANS
        `Art 26(2)(b) reading A: a governor or former governor of an entity registered at any time` person
    AND person's `governor while the entity was registered`

@ref Article 26(2)(b) (src:623)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.17 is the one read here)
      person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(2)(b) the person is a governor or former governor of a registered charity or of a formerly registered entity` interp person MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `when a person must have been a governor to fall within Article 26(2)(b)`
    WHEN `a governor at any time of an entity registered at any time falls within Article 26(2)(b)` THEN `Art 26(2)(b) reading A: a governor or former governor of an entity registered at any time` person
    WHEN `a person falls within Article 26(2)(b) only if a governor while the entity was registered` THEN `Art 26(2)(b) reading B: a governor while the entity was registered` person

@ref Article 26(2) (src:621-623)
@export Whether the person is one the Commissioner may demand information from under Article 26: a registered charity or formerly registered entity, or a governor or former governor of one. An applicant for registration, or a governor of an applicant that was never registered, is not within Article 26(2)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.17 is the one read here)
      person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of: how the person stands to the entity, and where the entity stands on the register
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(2) the person falls within Article 26(2)` interp person MEANS
        `Art 26(2)(a) the person is a registered charity or an entity that was formerly one` person
    OR  `Art 26(2)(b) the person is a governor or former governor of a registered charity or of a formerly registered entity` interp person

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(1): the power
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(1) -- the Commissioner may require information, a document or attendance`

-- T23: the demand serves one purpose, determining whether to serve a required steps notice.  26(10) names a second (an application for registration); information for an application
-- is asked for under Article 11(2)(f)(ii), which this module does not encode and which creates no offence (a request not met leaves the Commissioner unsatisfied, and 11(5) then requires refusal).
-- A demand for another purpose is not within 26(1).
-- src:618 | (1) The Commissioner may, by notice in writing served on a person falling within paragraph (2), require that person –
-- src:619 | (a) to provide to the Commissioner, at such time and place as may be specified in the notice, any information or document of a specified description that the Commissioner reasonably requires for the purpose of determining whether to serve a required steps notice; or
-- src:620 | (b) to attend at such place and time as may be specified in the notice and answer questions that the Commissioner reasonably requires the person to answer for that purpose.
@ref Article 26(1) (src:618-620)
@export Whether the Commissioner has the power under Article 26(1) to demand the information or attendance: the person is within Article 26(2), the purpose is determining whether to serve a required steps notice, and the Commissioner reasonably requires what is demanded
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.17 is the one read here)
      person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of
      demand IS A `Demand for information under Article 26(1)` @desc Why the Commissioner asks, and whether the Commissioner reasonably requires what is demanded for that purpose
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(1) the Commissioner has the power to demand` interp person demand MEANS
        `Art 26(2) the person falls within Article 26(2)` interp person
    AND demand's `purpose of the demand` EQUALS `determining whether to serve a required steps notice`
    AND demand's `the Commissioner reasonably requires what is demanded for that purpose`

-- The power is a permission of the Commissioner, exercised by notice in writing served on the person.  (a) and (b) are two things the notice may require, under one power;
-- an unexercised MAY is not a breach.  The notice must specify the time and place (26(1)(a), (b)): see `Art 26(1) the last day allowed by the notice`.
@ref Article 26(1) (src:618-620)
@export Whether the Commissioner may, by notice in writing, require the person to provide information or a document or to attend and answer questions; FULFILLED where the Commissioner has no such power over this person or for this purpose
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.17 is the one read here)
      person IS A `Person addressed by a notice under Article 26` @desc The person demanded of
      demand IS A `Demand for information under Article 26(1)` @desc Why the Commissioner asks, and whether the Commissioner reasonably requires what is demanded for that purpose
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(1) the Commissioner may require the person to provide information or attend` interp person demand MEANS
    IF   `Art 26(1) the Commissioner has the power to demand` interp person demand
    THEN PARTY `the Commissioner`
         MAY `serve a notice in writing requiring information, a document or attendance`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- IP27.  The decision whether to impose a requirement contained in a notice under Article 26 is a decision of the Commissioner that Article 33(3) lets the person served appeal.  The decision is a subject
-- and an outcome (domain.l4): imposing the requirement is "the Commissioner does what was sought or proposed", and deciding not to impose it is "the Commissioner refuses".
@ref Article 26(1) (src:618-620)
@export The decision of the Commissioner whether to impose a requirement contained in a notice under Article 26, as the decision that Article 33(3) lets the person served appeal: imposed is 'the Commissioner does what was sought or proposed', not imposed is 'the Commissioner refuses'
GIVEN `the Commissioner imposes the requirement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner decided to impose the requirement contained in the notice
GIVETH A `Decision of the Commissioner`
`Art 26 the decision of the Commissioner to impose a requirement` `the Commissioner imposes the requirement` MEANS
    `Decision of the Commissioner` WITH
        subject IS `whether to impose a requirement contained in a notice under Article 26`
        outcome IS (IF `the Commissioner imposes the requirement` THEN `the Commissioner does what was sought or proposed` ELSE `the Commissioner refuses`)

-- F8.7.  The time a notice specifies: "at such time and place as may be specified in the notice" (26(1)(a), (b)), "in a time specified in the notice" (27(2)).
-- A notice that fixes a date is read as written.  A notice that fixes a period of days leaves open whether the day of service is its first day: reading A (taken) does not count it,
-- reading B counts it.  (Whether the day of service is the day a notice is posted or the day it is received is G7's fork F7.7, Article 38; here the day of service is an input.)
@ref Article 26(1) (src:619-620)
GIVEN spec IS A `Time specified in a notice` @desc What the notice fixes: a date, or a number of days after the day of service
      served IS A DATE @desc The day on which the notice was served (Article 38 and G7's fork F7.7 fix it; it is an input here)
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 26(1) reading A: the last day allowed by the notice, the day of service not counted` spec served MEANS
    CONSIDER spec
    WHEN `a date fixed by the notice` d THEN d
    WHEN `a period of days fixed by the notice` n THEN served PLUS n

@ref Article 26(1) (src:619-620)
GIVEN spec IS A `Time specified in a notice` @desc What the notice fixes: a date, or a number of days after the day of service
      served IS A DATE @desc The day on which the notice was served
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 26(1) reading B: the last day allowed by the notice, the day of service counted` spec served MEANS
    CONSIDER spec
    WHEN `a date fixed by the notice` d THEN d
    WHEN `a period of days fixed by the notice` n THEN served PLUS (n MINUS 1)

@ref Article 26(1) (src:619-620)
@export The last day on which the thing a notice under Article 26(1) or Article 27(2) requires may be done, on the reading of how a period fixed by a notice is counted (default - the day of service is not counted)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.7 is the one read here)
      spec IS A `Time specified in a notice` @desc What the notice fixes: a date, or a number of days after the day of service
      `the date on which the notice was served` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the notice was served
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 26(1) the last day allowed by the notice` interp spec `the date on which the notice was served` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how a period fixed by a notice under Article 26 or 27 is counted`
    WHEN `the day of service is not counted in a period fixed by a notice` THEN `Art 26(1) reading A: the last day allowed by the notice, the day of service not counted` spec `the date on which the notice was served`
    WHEN `the day of service is the first day of a period fixed by a notice` THEN `Art 26(1) reading B: the last day allowed by the notice, the day of service counted` spec `the date on which the notice was served`

-- The same last day, as a number of days after the day of service: the WITHIN of a duty whose trace starts on the day of service.
@ref Article 26(1) (src:619-620)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.7 is the one read here)
      spec IS A `Time specified in a notice` @desc What the notice fixes: a date, or a number of days after the day of service
      `the date on which the notice was served` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the notice was served
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Art 26(1) the days allowed by the notice` interp spec `the date on which the notice was served` MEANS
    (`Art 26(1) the last day allowed by the notice` interp spec `the date on which the notice was served`) MINUS `the date on which the notice was served`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(3): a lien
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(3) -- a lien on a document`

-- The person must still provide the document: the Law says only that providing it is without prejudice to the lien.  It does not say that a claimed lien is an excuse;
-- whether it is a "reasonable excuse" under 26(7) is a judgement the caller supplies there.
-- src:624 | (3) If a person from whom provision of a document is required under paragraph (1) claims a lien on any such document, the provision is without prejudice to the lien.
@ref Article 26(3) (src:624)
GIVEN `the person claims a lien on the document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person from whom the document is required claims a lien on it
GIVETH AN `Effect on a lien of providing a document`
`Art 26(3) the effect on a lien of providing the document` `the person claims a lien on the document` MEANS
    IF   `the person claims a lien on the document`
    THEN `the lien is unaffected by the provision`
    ELSE `there is no lien for Article 26(3) to preserve`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(4): retain, copy, explain; where the document is
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(4) -- retaining or copying a document, and requiring an explanation or a statement of where it is`

-- src:625 | (4) If the Commissioner exercises a power under paragraph (1) to require a document to be provided, the Commissioner may –
-- src:626 | (a) if the document is provided, retain or take copies of it or extracts from it and require the person providing it, or any person who appears to be in possession of relevant information and to be a governor or employee of the registered charity, to provide an explanation of the document; and
-- src:627 | (b) if the document is not provided, require the person to whom the requirement was directed to state, to the best of the person’s knowledge and belief, where the document is.
@ref Article 26(4)(a) (src:625-626)
@export Whether the Commissioner may retain or take copies of or extracts from a document provided under a requirement in Article 26(1), and require an explanation of it; FULFILLED where the Commissioner has not exercised the power to require the document or the document was not provided
GIVEN `the Commissioner has exercised the power under Article 26(1) to require the document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner validly required the document under Article 26(1) (see Art 26(1) the Commissioner has the power to demand)
      `the document is provided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person provided the document
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(4)(a) the Commissioner may retain or copy the document and require an explanation` `the Commissioner has exercised the power under Article 26(1) to require the document` `the document is provided` MEANS
    IF   `the Commissioner has exercised the power under Article 26(1) to require the document` AND `the document is provided`
    THEN PARTY `the Commissioner`
         MAY `retain or copy a document provided and require an explanation of it`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- F8.19.  26(4)(a) lets the Commissioner require an explanation from the person who provided the document, or from "any person who appears to be in possession of relevant information
-- and to be a governor or employee of the registered charity".  26(2) reaches a formerly registered entity too, but 26(4)(a) says "the registered charity", a defined term (Article 1:
-- an entity entered for the time being in the general or restricted section).  Reading A (taken): the defined term is meant, so the charity must be registered now.
-- Reading B: the charity or formerly registered entity demanded of.
@ref Article 26(4)(a) (src:626)
GIVEN person IS A `Person asked for an explanation of a document` @desc Who is asked: whether the person provided the document, and what the Commissioner takes the person to be
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(4)(a) the person appears to be a governor or employee of a charity registered now` person MEANS
        person's `appears to be in possession of relevant information`
    AND person's `appears to be a governor or employee of the charity`
    AND person's `the charity is registered now`

@ref Article 26(4)(a) (src:626)
GIVEN person IS A `Person asked for an explanation of a document` @desc Who is asked
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(4)(a) the person appears to be a governor or employee of a charity registered now or formerly` person MEANS
        person's `appears to be in possession of relevant information`
    AND person's `appears to be a governor or employee of the charity`
    AND (   person's `the charity is registered now`
         OR person's `the entity was formerly a registered charity` )

@ref Article 26(4)(a) (src:626)
@export Whether the Commissioner may require the person to explain the document under Article 26(4)(a): the person who provided it, or a person who appears to hold relevant information and to be a governor or employee of the registered charity
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.19 is the one read here)
      person IS A `Person asked for an explanation of a document` @desc Who is asked: whether the person provided the document, and what the Commissioner takes the person to be
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(4)(a) an explanation of the document may be required of the person` interp person MEANS
    person's `provided the document`
    OR CONSIDER interp's `which charity Article 26(4)(a) speaks of`
       WHEN `the registered charity of Article 26(4)(a) is a charity registered now` THEN `Art 26(4)(a) the person appears to be a governor or employee of a charity registered now` person
       WHEN `the registered charity of Article 26(4)(a) is the charity or formerly registered entity demanded of` THEN `Art 26(4)(a) the person appears to be a governor or employee of a charity registered now or formerly` person

@ref Article 26(4)(b) (src:627)
@export Whether the Commissioner may require the person to whom the requirement was directed to state, to the best of the person's knowledge and belief, where a document that was not provided is; FULFILLED where the document was provided or the power under Article 26(1) was not exercised
GIVEN `the Commissioner has exercised the power under Article 26(1) to require the document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner validly required the document under Article 26(1)
      `the document is provided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person provided the document
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(4)(b) the Commissioner may require a statement of where the document is` `the Commissioner has exercised the power under Article 26(1) to require the document` `the document is provided` MEANS
    IF   `the Commissioner has exercised the power under Article 26(1) to require the document` AND NOT `the document is provided`
    THEN PARTY `the Commissioner`
         MAY `require a statement of where a document that was not provided is`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(5): how long what is retained may be kept
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(5) -- the retention period: the longer of one year and the end of proceedings commenced within it`

-- P23.  Three questions the text leaves open about "a period of one year": the day it starts (F8.1), whether it ends on the anniversary or the day before (F8.9),
-- and what a year from 29 February is (F8.10).  Whether proceedings commenced on the last day of the year are "within that period" follows from F8.9:
-- the last day is in the period, so they are.
-- src:628 | (5) A document, copy or extract retained or taken under paragraph (4)(a) may be retained for whichever is the longer of –
-- src:629 | (a) a period of one year; and
-- src:630 | (b) if within that period proceedings to which the document is relevant are commenced against any person, until the conclusion of those proceedings.
@ref Article 26(5)(a) (src:628-629)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.1 is the one read here)
      facts IS A `Facts of the retention of a document under Article 26(5)` @desc The days the document was provided or retained and of the notice, and the proceedings
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 26(5)(a) the first day of the retention year` interp facts MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `when the retention year of Article 26(5) starts`
    WHEN `the year of Article 26(5) runs from the day the document is provided or retained` THEN facts's `day the document was provided or retained`
    WHEN `the year of Article 26(5) runs from the day of the notice` THEN facts's `day of the notice`

@ref Article 26(5)(a) (src:629)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.10 is the one read here)
      start IS A DATE @desc The day the year starts
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 26(5)(a) the anniversary of the day the year starts` interp start MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how a year is added to 29 February under Article 26(5)`
    WHEN `a year of Article 26(5) from 29 February ends on 28 February` THEN `add years` start 1
    WHEN `a year of Article 26(5) from 29 February ends on 1 March` THEN Date (DATE_DAY start) (DATE_MONTH start) (DATE_YEAR start PLUS 1)

@ref Article 26(5)(a) (src:629)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.9 and F8.10 are the ones read here)
      start IS A DATE @desc The day the year starts
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 26(5)(a) the last day of the retention year` interp start MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `where the retention year of Article 26(5) ends`
    WHEN `the year of Article 26(5) ends on the anniversary of the day it starts` THEN `Art 26(5)(a) the anniversary of the day the year starts` interp start
    WHEN `the year of Article 26(5) ends on the day before the anniversary of the day it starts` THEN (`Art 26(5)(a) the anniversary of the day the year starts` interp start) MINUS 1

-- (b) arises only if proceedings to which the document is relevant are commenced within the year (on or after its first day, on or before its last).  Then the document may be kept
-- for whichever is longer: the year, or until the proceedings conclude.  Proceedings commenced before the year starts, or after it ends, do not extend it.
@ref Article 26(5)(b) (src:630)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.1, F8.9 and F8.10 are the ones read here)
      facts IS A `Facts of the retention of a document under Article 26(5)` @desc The days the document was provided or retained and of the notice, and the proceedings
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(5)(b) proceedings were commenced within the retention year` interp facts MEANS
    CONSIDER facts's `day proceedings were commenced`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST commenced THEN
            commenced AT LEAST `Art 26(5)(a) the first day of the retention year` interp facts
        AND commenced AT MOST `Art 26(5)(a) the last day of the retention year` interp (`Art 26(5)(a) the first day of the retention year` interp facts)

@ref Article 26(5)(b) (src:630)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.1, F8.9 and F8.10 are the ones read here)
      facts IS A `Facts of the retention of a document under Article 26(5)` @desc The days the document was provided or retained and of the notice, and the proceedings
GIVETH AN `End of the retention of a document under Article 26(5)`
`Art 26(5)(b) the end of the retention when proceedings were commenced within the year` interp facts MEANS
    CONSIDER facts's `day proceedings concluded`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `retention is permitted until proceedings conclude, and they have not concluded`
    WHEN JUST concluded THEN `retention is permitted up to and including` (`the later of` concluded (`Art 26(5)(a) the last day of the retention year` interp (`Art 26(5)(a) the first day of the retention year` interp facts)))

@ref Article 26(5) (src:628-630)
@export Until when the Commissioner may retain a document, copy or extract taken under Article 26(4)(a): the longer of one year and, if proceedings to which it is relevant are commenced within the year, the conclusion of those proceedings (default - the year runs from the day the document is provided or retained and ends on the anniversary)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.1, F8.9 and F8.10 are the ones read here)
      facts IS A `Facts of the retention of a document under Article 26(5)` @desc The days the document was provided or retained and of the notice, and the proceedings
GIVETH AN `End of the retention of a document under Article 26(5)`
`Art 26(5) the Commissioner may keep the document until` interp facts MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `Art 26(5)(b) proceedings were commenced within the retention year` interp facts
        THEN `Art 26(5)(b) the end of the retention when proceedings were commenced within the year` interp facts
        OTHERWISE `retention is permitted up to and including` (`Art 26(5)(a) the last day of the retention year` interp (`Art 26(5)(a) the first day of the retention year` interp facts))

@ref Article 26(5) (src:628-630)
@export Whether the Commissioner may still retain the document, copy or extract on the given day under Article 26(5)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.1, F8.9 and F8.10 are the ones read here)
      facts IS A `Facts of the retention of a document under Article 26(5)` @desc The days the document was provided or retained and of the notice, and the proceedings
      day IS A DATE @desc The day on which retention is asked about
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(5) retention is permitted on` interp facts day MEANS
    CONSIDER `Art 26(5) the Commissioner may keep the document until` interp facts
    WHEN `retention is permitted up to and including` last THEN day AT MOST last
    WHEN `retention is permitted until proceedings conclude, and they have not concluded` THEN TRUE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(6): a copy for a person who needs the document for business
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(6) -- a copy of a retained document, as soon as practicable`

-- The sentence is passive ("must be supplied with a copy") and names no supplier.  Only the Commissioner retains the document under 26(4)(a), so the duty is typed on the Commissioner;
-- that is the encoder's inference, recorded in FRAGMENT-G8.md as an open question, and it has no second candidate to make a fork of.
-- P24, F8.2.  The Law gives no number of days.  Reading A (taken): "as soon as practicable" is a judgement the caller supplies, so the duty carries no WITHIN;
-- reading B: a number of days from the request, which the caller supplies, becomes the WITHIN (the day of the request not counted); reading C: the same with the day of the request counted as the
-- first of the days, so the WITHIN is one day shorter.
-- src:631 | (6) A person who requires a document retained under paragraph (4)(a) for the purpose of the person’s business, and who requests that document, must be supplied with a copy as soon as practicable.
@ref Article 26(6) (src:631)
@export Whether the Commissioner must supply a copy of the retained document to a person who needs it for the person's business and has asked for it, as soon as practicable (default - no number of days; with the days reading the copy is due within the number of days given); FULFILLED where the person does not need it for business or has not asked
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.2 is the one read here)
      request IS A `Request for a copy of a retained document` @desc Whether the person needs the document for business and has asked for it, and when
      days IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc Only read on the days reading of F8.2: the number of days from the request within which the copy must be supplied; NOTHING if none has been supplied by the caller
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(6) the Commissioner must supply a copy of the retained document` interp request days MEANS
    IF       request's `the person requires the document for the purpose of the person's business`
         AND request's `the person has requested the document`
    THEN CONSIDER interp's `how promptness under Article 26(6) is judged`
         WHEN `promptness under Article 26(6) is a judgement the caller supplies` THEN `Art 26(6) reading A: the duty to supply a copy`
         WHEN `promptness under Article 26(6) is a number of days from the request` THEN `Art 26(6) reading B: the duty to supply a copy within the days` days
         WHEN `promptness under Article 26(6) is a number of days counting the day of the request as the first` THEN `Art 26(6) reading C: the duty to supply a copy within the days, the day of the request counted` days
    ELSE FULFILLED

@ref Article 26(6) (src:631)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(6) reading A: the duty to supply a copy` MEANS
    PARTY `the Commissioner`
    MUST `supply a copy of a retained document`
    HENCE FULFILLED

@ref Article 26(6) (src:631)
GIVEN days IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The number of days from the request within which the copy must be supplied
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(6) reading B: the duty to supply a copy within the days` days MEANS
    CONSIDER days
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Article 26(6) gives no number of days, and none was supplied for the days reading"
    WHEN JUST n THEN
        PARTY `the Commissioner`
        MUST `supply a copy of a retained document`
        WITHIN n
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY `the Commissioner` BECAUSE "Article 26(6): the copy was not supplied within the number of days from the request"

@ref Article 26(6) (src:631)
GIVEN days IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The number of days from the request within which the copy must be supplied, the day of the request being the first of them
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(6) reading C: the duty to supply a copy within the days, the day of the request counted` days MEANS
    CONSIDER days
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Article 26(6) gives no number of days, and none was supplied for the days reading"
    WHEN JUST n THEN
        PARTY `the Commissioner`
        MUST `supply a copy of a retained document`
        WITHIN (n MINUS 1)
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY `the Commissioner` BECAUSE "Article 26(6): the copy was not supplied within the number of days counting the day of the request"

@ref Article 26(6) (src:631)
@export Whether the copy of the retained document was supplied as soon as practicable: on the judgement reading the caller's judgement, on the days reading whether it was supplied within the number of days from the request. FALSE if no copy has been supplied
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.2 is the one read here)
      request IS A `Request for a copy of a retained document` @desc Whether the person needs the document for business and has asked for it, and when
      days IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc Only read on the days reading of F8.2: the number of days from the request; NOTHING if none was supplied by the caller
      `the copy was supplied as soon as practicable in the caller's judgement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only read on the judgement reading of F8.2: the caller's judgement that the copy was supplied as soon as practicable
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(6) the copy was supplied as soon as practicable` interp request days `the copy was supplied as soon as practicable in the caller's judgement` MEANS
    CONSIDER request's `day the copy was supplied`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST supplied THEN
        CONSIDER interp's `how promptness under Article 26(6) is judged`
        WHEN `promptness under Article 26(6) is a judgement the caller supplies` THEN `the copy was supplied as soon as practicable in the caller's judgement`
        WHEN `promptness under Article 26(6) is a number of days from the request` THEN
            CONSIDER days
            WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Article 26(6) gives no number of days, and none was supplied for the days reading"
            WHEN JUST n THEN (supplied MINUS request's `day of the request`) AT MOST n
        WHEN `promptness under Article 26(6) is a number of days counting the day of the request as the first` THEN
            CONSIDER days
            WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Article 26(6) gives no number of days, and none was supplied for the days reading"
            WHEN JUST n THEN (supplied MINUS request's `day of the request`) AT MOST (n MINUS 1)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(7), 26(8): contravening a requirement
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(7) and 26(8) -- contravening a requirement, and legal professional privilege`


-- The requirement binds the person to do what is required in the time the notice specifies.  The days counted from the day of service are those of `Art 26(1) the last day allowed by the notice` (F8.7).
-- The offence is not committed by a contravention that has a reasonable excuse: that is the offence rule below.
-- src:632 | (7) A person who without reasonable excuse contravenes a requirement imposed on the person under paragraph (1) or (4), commits an offence and is liable to imprisonment for a term of 3 months and to a fine of level 3 on the standard scale.
@ref Article 26(7) (src:632)
@export Whether the person must comply with a requirement imposed under Article 26(1) or (4) within the time the notice specifies, counted from the day of service (default - the day of service is not counted); a breach is an offence unless the person has a reasonable excuse (3 months and a fine of level 3)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 26 to 36` @desc The readings of the forks of Articles 26 to 36 (F8.7 is the one read here)
      spec IS A `Time specified in a notice` @desc What the notice fixes: a date, or a number of days after the day of service
      `the date on which the notice was served` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the notice was served; the trace of the duty starts on this day
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(7) the person must comply with the requirement` interp spec `the date on which the notice was served` MEANS
    PARTY `any person`
    MUST `comply with a requirement imposed under Article 26`
    WITHIN (`Art 26(1) the days allowed by the notice` interp spec `the date on which the notice was served`)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `any person` BECAUSE "Article 26(7): the requirement imposed under Article 26(1) or (4) was not complied with in the time the notice specifies (an offence unless the person has a reasonable excuse)"

@ref Article 26(7) (src:632)
@export Whether the person commits the offence of contravening a requirement imposed under Article 26(1) or (4): the requirement was validly imposed and covers what is withheld, the person did not do what was required, and the person has no reasonable excuse (3 months and a fine of level 3)
GIVEN facts IS A `Facts of a contravention under Article 26(7)` @desc Whether the requirement was validly imposed, whether the person complied, whether what is withheld is privileged, and whether the person has a reasonable excuse
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(7) the person commits an offence` facts MEANS
        facts's `the requirement was validly imposed under Article 26(1) or (4)`
    AND `Art 26(8) a requirement under Article 26 extends to what is withheld` (facts's `what is withheld is subject to legal professional privilege`)
    AND NOT facts's `the person did what was required`
    AND NOT facts's `the person has a reasonable excuse`

-- The penalty is data: NOTHING where no offence is committed.
@ref Article 26(7) (src:632)
GIVEN facts IS A `Facts of a contravention under Article 26(7)` @desc The facts of the contravention
GIVETH A MAYBE `Penalty provided`
`Art 26(7) the penalty the person is liable to` facts MEANS
    IF   `Art 26(7) the person commits an offence` facts
    THEN JUST `Art 26(7) the penalty provided for the offence`
    ELSE NOTHING

-- 26(8): a limit on every requirement under the Article.  The caller supplies whether the person would be entitled to refuse on grounds of legal professional privilege.
-- src:633 | (8) Nothing in this Article is to be construed as requiring the disclosure or production by a person to the Commissioner of information or documents that the person would in an action in court be entitled to refuse to disclose or produce on the grounds of legal professional privilege.
@ref Article 26(8) (src:633)
GIVEN `what is withheld is subject to legal professional privilege` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person would in an action in court be entitled to refuse to disclose or produce it on grounds of legal professional privilege
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(8) a requirement under Article 26 extends to what is withheld` `what is withheld is subject to legal professional privilege` MEANS
    NOT `what is withheld is subject to legal professional privilege`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(9): the statement cannot be used by the prosecution
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(9) -- use of a statement made in compliance`

-- The bar is on the prosecution, against the person who made the statement, in criminal proceedings, except proceedings under 26(7) or 26(10).  Outside it - a statement used against
-- someone else, or in proceedings that are not criminal - Article 26(9) says nothing, so "may be used" below means only that Article 26(9) does not bar it; other law may.
-- src:634 | (9) A statement made by a person in compliance with a requirement imposed under this Article may not be used by the prosecution in evidence against the person in any criminal proceedings except proceedings under paragraph (7) or (10).
@ref Article 26(9) (src:634)
GIVEN statement IS A `Statement made under Article 26` @desc Whether it was made in compliance with a requirement under Article 26, against whom it is tendered, and in which proceedings
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(9) the prosecution is barred from using the statement` statement MEANS
        statement's `made in compliance with a requirement imposed under Article 26`
    AND statement's `tendered against the person who made it`
    AND statement's `proceedings` EQUALS `any other criminal proceedings`

@ref Article 26(9) (src:634)
@export Whether the prosecution may use a statement made in compliance with a requirement under Article 26 in evidence against the person who made it: it may not in criminal proceedings other than proceedings under Article 26(7) or (10); TRUE means only that Article 26(9) does not bar it
GIVEN statement IS A `Statement made under Article 26` @desc Whether it was made in compliance with a requirement under Article 26, against whom it is tendered, and in which proceedings
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(9) the statement may be used by the prosecution` statement MEANS
    NOT `Art 26(9) the prosecution is barred from using the statement` statement

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 26(10), 26(11): false information
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `26(10) and 26(11) -- providing false information`

-- The prohibition: the person SHANT provide information to the Commissioner that the person knows to be false in a material particular, intending it to be used for an application
-- for registration or for determining whether to serve a required steps notice.  Knowledge and intent are properties of the act, carried on it and tested by PROVIDED; providing
-- information without both is not the prohibited act.
-- src:635 | (10) A person commits an offence, and is liable to imprisonment for a term of 2 years and to a fine, if the person provides information to the Commissioner, knowing that it is false in a material particular and intending it to be used by the Commissioner for the purpose of an application for registration or for the purpose of determining whether to serve a required steps notice.
@ref Article 26(10) (src:635)
@export The prohibition in Article 26(10): a person must not provide information to the Commissioner knowing it to be false in a material particular and intending it to be used for an application for registration or for determining whether to serve a required steps notice (2 years and a fine)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 26 to 31`
`Art 26(10) the person must not give false information` MEANS
    PARTY `any person`
    SHANT `provide information to the Commissioner` `known false` `intended use` PROVIDED `known false` AND `intended use`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `any person` BECAUSE "Article 26(10): the person provided information to the Commissioner, knowing it to be false in a material particular and intending it to be used for an application for registration or for determining whether to serve a required steps notice"

-- 26(11): whether the information is in a document, and whether it was provided under Article 26, are irrelevant to the offence.  The two facts are on the record so that a caller
-- can state them; this rule does not read them.
-- src:636 | (11) For the purpose of paragraph (10) it is irrelevant whether the information –
-- src:637 | (a) is contained in a document or not; or
-- src:638 | (b) is provided under this Article or not.
@ref Article 26(10) (src:635)
@ref Article 26(11) (src:636-638)
@export Whether the person commits the offence of providing false information under Article 26(10): the person provided information to the Commissioner, knowing it to be false in a material particular, intending it to be used for an application for registration or for determining whether to serve a required steps notice; whether it is in a document or provided under Article 26 is irrelevant (2 years and a fine)
GIVEN facts IS A `Facts of providing information under Article 26(10)` @desc What the person provided, knew and intended
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 26(10) the person commits an offence` facts MEANS
        facts's `the person provided information to the Commissioner`
    AND facts's `known by the person to be false in a material particular`
    AND (   facts's `intended for an application for registration`
         OR facts's `intended for deciding whether to serve a required steps notice` )

@ref Article 26(10) (src:635)
GIVEN facts IS A `Facts of providing information under Article 26(10)` @desc What the person provided, knew and intended
GIVETH A MAYBE `Penalty provided`
`Art 26(10) the penalty the person is liable to` facts MEANS
    IF   `Art 26(10) the person commits an offence` facts
    THEN JUST `Art 26(10) the penalty provided for the offence`
    ELSE NOTHING
